 "readme": "One entry per name that data.json files under a single key but that may cover more than one player. Read by scripts/factoids.py only: build_data.py and data.json do not know this file exists. Candidates are names whose career has a gap longer than 4 seasons. split: true means two people, split: false means one player who came back after a spell away. confirmed says a human has checked the entry. An unconfirmed split holds back every season in an earlier segment, as a subject and as a previous holder, so no sentence carries the wrong man's name. A confirmed split prints each segment's own display_name and counts each segment as its own man, so \"his own mark\" never reaches across the two; a segment whose display_name is null is a man whose name is not known and stays held back. Career-level claims stay closed for every split key either way, because career_earnings on a merged record sums two men. An unconfirmed split: false entry keeps the career-level exclusion its gap triggered: a long gap under one name is not proof of one person. Confirming it is what lets the name rank as one career."
